{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "devsweep",
          "version": "0.0.0-test",
          "semanticVersion": "0.0.0-test",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/Frank1738/devsweep",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "workflow/untrusted-checkout",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Runs pull request code with secrets"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Workflows triggered by pull_request_target run with repository secrets and a GITHUB_TOKEN that can write. Checking out the pull request head lets anyone who opens a PR from a fork run their own code with those secrets (a \"pwn request\")."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Workflows triggered by pull_request_target run with repository secrets and a GITHUB_TOKEN that can write. Checking out the pull request head lets anyone who opens a PR from a fork run their own code with those secrets (a \"pwn request\").\n\nHow to fix: Switch to the pull_request trigger, or split the work: build untrusted code in a pull_request workflow with no secrets, then act on its results in a separate workflow_run workflow.",
                "markdown": "Workflows triggered by pull\\_request\\_target run with repository secrets and a GITHUB\\_TOKEN that can write. Checking out the pull request head lets anyone who opens a PR from a fork run their own code with those secrets (a \"pwn request\").\n\n## How to fix\n\nSwitch to the pull\\_request trigger, or split the work: build untrusted code in a pull\\_request workflow with no secrets, then act on its results in a separate workflow\\_run workflow."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "workflow/pull-request-target",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Uses the pull_request_target trigger"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "pull_request_target runs in the context of the base repository, with secrets and a GITHUB_TOKEN that can write, even for pull requests from forks. It is safe only if the workflow never checks out, builds or runs code from the pull request."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "pull_request_target runs in the context of the base repository, with secrets and a GITHUB_TOKEN that can write, even for pull requests from forks. It is safe only if the workflow never checks out, builds or runs code from the pull request.\n\nHow to fix: Use the pull_request trigger unless the workflow needs secrets or write access. If it does, never check out or run code from the pull request.",
                "markdown": "pull\\_request\\_target runs in the context of the base repository, with secrets and a GITHUB\\_TOKEN that can write, even for pull requests from forks. It is safe only if the workflow never checks out, builds or runs code from the pull request.\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse the pull\\_request trigger unless the workflow needs secrets or write access. If it does, never check out or run code from the pull request."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "workflow/unpinned-action",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unpinned action"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Tags and branches can be moved to point at different code at any time. If the owner of a third-party action is compromised, every workflow that references it by tag runs the attacker's code with that workflow's secrets. Only a full commit SHA is immutable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Tags and branches can be moved to point at different code at any time. If the owner of a third-party action is compromised, every workflow that references it by tag runs the attacker's code with that workflow's secrets. Only a full commit SHA is immutable.\n\nHow to fix: Pin third-party actions and reusable workflows to a full 40-character commit SHA, with the version in a comment (uses: owner/action@<sha> # v1.2.3), and pin container images by digest (docker://image@sha256:<digest>). Dependabot and Renovate can keep the pins up to date.",
                "markdown": "Tags and branches can be moved to point at different code at any time. If the owner of a third-party action is compromised, every workflow that references it by tag runs the attacker's code with that workflow's secrets. Only a full commit SHA is immutable.\n\n## How to fix\n\nPin third-party actions and reusable workflows to a full 40-character commit SHA, with the version in a comment (uses: owner/action@\\<sha\\> # v1.2.3), and pin container images by digest (docker://image@sha256:\\<digest\\>). Dependabot and Renovate can keep the pins up to date."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "workflow/write-all-permissions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Token can write to everything"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "permissions: write-all gives the GITHUB_TOKEN write access to code, packages, releases, issues, pull requests and more. Any compromised step can use it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "permissions: write-all gives the GITHUB_TOKEN write access to code, packages, releases, issues, pull requests and more. Any compromised step can use it.\n\nHow to fix: List only the scopes the workflow needs, e.g. \"contents: read\", and grant write scopes only to the jobs that need them.",
                "markdown": "permissions: write-all gives the GITHUB\\_TOKEN write access to code, packages, releases, issues, pull requests and more. Any compromised step can use it.\n\n## How to fix\n\nList only the scopes the workflow needs, e.g. \"contents: read\", and grant write scopes only to the jobs that need them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "workflow/missing-permissions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Token permissions not limited"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without a permissions block, jobs get the default GITHUB_TOKEN permissions from the repository or organization settings. On older repositories and many organizations that default is read and write for every scope, so any compromised step can push code, edit releases or approve pull requests."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a permissions block, jobs get the default GITHUB_TOKEN permissions from the repository or organization settings. On older repositories and many organizations that default is read and write for every scope, so any compromised step can push code, edit releases or approve pull requests.\n\nHow to fix: Set \"permissions: contents: read\" at the top of the workflow, and grant extra scopes only to the jobs that need them.",
                "markdown": "Without a permissions block, jobs get the default GITHUB\\_TOKEN permissions from the repository or organization settings. On older repositories and many organizations that default is read and write for every scope, so any compromised step can push code, edit releases or approve pull requests.\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet \"permissions: contents: read\" at the top of the workflow, and grant extra scopes only to the jobs that need them."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "workflow/force-push",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Force push"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A force push replaces history on the remote instead of adding to it. A workflow that force-pushes holds a token that can rewrite branches or move tags, and anyone who compromises the workflow, through a malicious dependency, action or injected script, can use it to hide what they changed. Severity depends on the target: high for main, master, tags and mirrors, medium for other branches, low for deploy branches such as gh-pages."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A force push replaces history on the remote instead of adding to it. A workflow that force-pushes holds a token that can rewrite branches or move tags, and anyone who compromises the workflow, through a malicious dependency, action or injected script, can use it to hide what they changed. Severity depends on the target: high for main, master, tags and mirrors, medium for other branches, low for deploy branches such as gh-pages.\n\nHow to fix: Push without force where possible, so a diverged branch fails instead of being overwritten. Block force pushes on the default branch and release tags with a ruleset, and allow them only where a workflow needs them, such as a gh-pages deploy branch.",
                "markdown": "A force push replaces history on the remote instead of adding to it. A workflow that force-pushes holds a token that can rewrite branches or move tags, and anyone who compromises the workflow, through a malicious dependency, action or injected script, can use it to hide what they changed. Severity depends on the target: high for main, master, tags and mirrors, medium for other branches, low for deploy branches such as gh-pages.\n\n## How to fix\n\nPush without force where possible, so a diverged branch fails instead of being overwritten. Block force pushes on the default branch and release tags with a ruleset, and allow them only where a workflow needs them, such as a gh-pages deploy branch."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "workflow"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "repo/force-push-allowed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Force pushes allowed"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without a ruleset or branch protection that blocks it, anyone with write access, or a stolen token with write access, can force-push the default branch or move a release tag. That rewrites history and can point a trusted tag at malicious code, which is how the tj-actions/changed-files attack in March 2025 reached thousands of repositories."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a ruleset or branch protection that blocks it, anyone with write access, or a stolen token with write access, can force-push the default branch or move a release tag. That rewrites history and can point a trusted tag at malicious code, which is how the tj-actions/changed-files attack in March 2025 reached thousands of repositories.\n\nHow to fix: In Settings → Rules → Rulesets, add a branch ruleset for the default branch with \"Block force pushes\" and \"Restrict deletions\", and a tag ruleset for release tags (e.g. refs/tags/v*) with \"Restrict updates\" and \"Restrict deletions\".",
                "markdown": "Without a ruleset or branch protection that blocks it, anyone with write access, or a stolen token with write access, can force-push the default branch or move a release tag. That rewrites history and can point a trusted tag at malicious code, which is how the tj-actions/changed-files attack in March 2025 reached thousands of repositories.\n\n## How to fix\n\nIn Settings → Rules → Rulesets, add a branch ruleset for the default branch with \"Block force pushes\" and \"Restrict deletions\", and a tag ruleset for release tags (e.g. refs/tags/v\\*) with \"Restrict updates\" and \"Restrict deletions\"."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "repo"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "repo/force-push-detected",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Recent force push"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Someone force-pushed the default branch in the last 90 days, replacing part of its history. That can be routine cleanup, but it is also how a stolen token hides what it changed. GitHub keeps a record of force pushes for branches only, so moved tags cannot be detected this way."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Someone force-pushed the default branch in the last 90 days, replacing part of its history. That can be routine cleanup, but it is also how a stolen token hides what it changed. GitHub keeps a record of force pushes for branches only, so moved tags cannot be detected this way.\n\nHow to fix: Open the repository's Activity view on GitHub, compare the commits before and after the push, and confirm who made it and why. Then accept the finding in devsweep.config.json with that reason, and block future force pushes with a ruleset.",
                "markdown": "Someone force-pushed the default branch in the last 90 days, replacing part of its history. That can be routine cleanup, but it is also how a stolen token hides what it changed. GitHub keeps a record of force pushes for branches only, so moved tags cannot be detected this way.\n\n## How to fix\n\nOpen the repository's Activity view on GitHub, compare the commits before and after the push, and confirm who made it and why. Then accept the finding in devsweep.config.json with that reason, and block future force pushes with a ruleset."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "repo"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code/autorun-task",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Task runs when the folder opens"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A VS Code task with \"runOn\": \"folderOpen\" runs a command as soon as someone opens the folder, before they've read any of the code. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way in fake take-home assignments and infected repositories. Some real projects use it to start a file watcher, so a plain auto-run task is medium. It is critical when the task also runs node, a shell or python on a file that isn't a script (such as a font), pipes a download into a shell, hides its output, or contacts a vercel.app /settings/ address. It is high when .vscode/settings.json also sets \"task.allowAutomaticTasks\" to \"on\", which tries to let tasks run without VS Code asking first."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A VS Code task with \"runOn\": \"folderOpen\" runs a command as soon as someone opens the folder, before they've read any of the code. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way in fake take-home assignments and infected repositories. Some real projects use it to start a file watcher, so a plain auto-run task is medium. It is critical when the task also runs node, a shell or python on a file that isn't a script (such as a font), pipes a download into a shell, hides its output, or contacts a vercel.app /settings/ address. It is high when .vscode/settings.json also sets \"task.allowAutomaticTasks\" to \"on\", which tries to let tasks run without VS Code asking first.\n\nHow to fix: Open .vscode/tasks.json in a plain text editor, without opening the folder in VS Code, and read the task's \"command\" and \"args\" to see what it runs. If you don't recognise the task, delete it from the \"tasks\" list, and check the files it names and the rest of the repository before you open it in VS Code. If the project really needs it, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json. Either way, set \"task.allowAutomaticTasks\": \"off\" in your own VS Code user settings, so no folder can run tasks without asking you.",
                "markdown": "A VS Code task with \"runOn\": \"folderOpen\" runs a command as soon as someone opens the folder, before they've read any of the code. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way in fake take-home assignments and infected repositories. Some real projects use it to start a file watcher, so a plain auto-run task is medium. It is critical when the task also runs node, a shell or python on a file that isn't a script (such as a font), pipes a download into a shell, hides its output, or contacts a vercel.app /settings/ address. It is high when .vscode/settings.json also sets \"task.allowAutomaticTasks\" to \"on\", which tries to let tasks run without VS Code asking first.\n\n## How to fix\n\nOpen .vscode/tasks.json in a plain text editor, without opening the folder in VS Code, and read the task's \"command\" and \"args\" to see what it runs. If you don't recognise the task, delete it from the \"tasks\" list, and check the files it names and the rest of the repository before you open it in VS Code. If the project really needs it, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json. Either way, set \"task.allowAutomaticTasks\": \"off\" in your own VS Code user settings, so no folder can run tasks without asking you."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code/hidden-padding",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Code hidden after a long run of spaces"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A line has 64 or more spaces or tabs in a row, then more code. Code that far to the right is off-screen in most editors and in GitHub's diff view, so a reviewer doesn't see it. Every known variant of the PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way, at the end of a real file such as a build config. Critical when the hidden code also runs text as code (eval, new Function), decodes base64 (atob, Buffer.from) or sets a global variable. Markdown, .txt and .csv files are skipped, and so is padding followed by a comment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A line has 64 or more spaces or tabs in a row, then more code. Code that far to the right is off-screen in most editors and in GitHub's diff view, so a reviewer doesn't see it. Every known variant of the PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way, at the end of a real file such as a build config. Critical when the hidden code also runs text as code (eval, new Function), decodes base64 (atob, Buffer.from) or sets a global variable. Markdown, .txt and .csv files are skipped, and so is padding followed by a comment.\n\nHow to fix: Open the file in a plain text editor with line wrapping on, or scroll to the line's end, and read what follows the spaces. If you didn't write it, don't build or run the project: delete the hidden code, check the file's git history for who added it, and check the rest of the repository and your machine. If the long line is intended, such as generated data, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json.",
                "markdown": "A line has 64 or more spaces or tabs in a row, then more code. Code that far to the right is off-screen in most editors and in GitHub's diff view, so a reviewer doesn't see it. Every known variant of the PolinRider malware campaign hides its loader this way, at the end of a real file such as a build config. Critical when the hidden code also runs text as code (eval, new Function), decodes base64 (atob, Buffer.from) or sets a global variable. Markdown, .txt and .csv files are skipped, and so is padding followed by a comment.\n\n## How to fix\n\nOpen the file in a plain text editor with line wrapping on, or scroll to the line's end, and read what follows the spaces. If you didn't write it, don't build or run the project: delete the hidden code, check the file's git history for who added it, and check the rest of the repository and your machine. If the long line is intended, such as generated data, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code/disguised-file",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Font file that isn't a font"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A file named like a font (.woff2, .woff, .ttf, .otf or .eot) whose first bytes are not any font format, or a font-like name no font uses, such as fa-solid-400.llf. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its JavaScript in fake Font Awesome fonts, then runs them with node from a VS Code task or an npm script. Critical when the file is mostly readable text, or a command in .vscode/tasks.json or package.json names it. Empty files and Git LFS pointers (the small text files left in place of fonts stored in Git LFS) are skipped."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A file named like a font (.woff2, .woff, .ttf, .otf or .eot) whose first bytes are not any font format, or a font-like name no font uses, such as fa-solid-400.llf. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its JavaScript in fake Font Awesome fonts, then runs them with node from a VS Code task or an npm script. Critical when the file is mostly readable text, or a command in .vscode/tasks.json or package.json names it. Empty files and Git LFS pointers (the small text files left in place of fonts stored in Git LFS) are skipped.\n\nHow to fix: Don't run the file. Open it in a plain text editor: a real font is unreadable binary data. If it holds code, delete it, remove any task or script that runs it, and check the repository's history for who added it. If you ran the project, treat your machine as compromised and rotate tokens from a clean device. If the file is a real font in an unusual format, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json.",
                "markdown": "A file named like a font (.woff2, .woff, .ttf, .otf or .eot) whose first bytes are not any font format, or a font-like name no font uses, such as fa-solid-400.llf. The PolinRider malware campaign hides its JavaScript in fake Font Awesome fonts, then runs them with node from a VS Code task or an npm script. Critical when the file is mostly readable text, or a command in .vscode/tasks.json or package.json names it. Empty files and Git LFS pointers (the small text files left in place of fonts stored in Git LFS) are skipped.\n\n## How to fix\n\nDon't run the file. Open it in a plain text editor: a real font is unreadable binary data. If it holds code, delete it, remove any task or script that runs it, and check the repository's history for who added it. If you ran the project, treat your machine as compromised and rotate tokens from a clean device. If the file is a real font in an unusual format, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code/propagation-script",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PolinRider propagation script"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PolinRider malware spreads with batch scripts named temp_auto_push.bat and temp_interactive_push.bat: they rewrite git history and force-push the infection to every repository the infected machine can write to. Either file anywhere in a project is critical. A .gitignore line that is exactly config.bat, temp_auto_push.bat or branch_structure.json is medium: it doesn't mean the payload is here, but suggests a contributor's machine was infected and pushed to this repository."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The PolinRider malware spreads with batch scripts named temp_auto_push.bat and temp_interactive_push.bat: they rewrite git history and force-push the infection to every repository the infected machine can write to. Either file anywhere in a project is critical. A .gitignore line that is exactly config.bat, temp_auto_push.bat or branch_structure.json is medium: it doesn't mean the payload is here, but suggests a contributor's machine was infected and pushed to this repository.\n\nHow to fix: Don't run the script. Delete it, and treat the machine that created it as compromised: rotate its GitHub tokens and SSH keys from a clean device. Check the repository for force pushes (devsweep's repo/force-push-detected rule does this with a GitHub token) and compare recent commits with what their authors remember writing.",
                "markdown": "The PolinRider malware spreads with batch scripts named temp\\_auto\\_push.bat and temp\\_interactive\\_push.bat: they rewrite git history and force-push the infection to every repository the infected machine can write to. Either file anywhere in a project is critical. A .gitignore line that is exactly config.bat, temp\\_auto\\_push.bat or branch\\_structure.json is medium: it doesn't mean the payload is here, but suggests a contributor's machine was infected and pushed to this repository.\n\n## How to fix\n\nDon't run the script. Delete it, and treat the machine that created it as compromised: rotate its GitHub tokens and SSH keys from a clean device. Check the repository for force pushes (devsweep's repo/force-push-detected rule does this with a GitHub token) and compare recent commits with what their authors remember writing."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "code/known-marker",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Known PolinRider marker"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The file contains a string proven to come from the PolinRider malware campaign: an obfuscator marker, a campaign tag, an XOR key, a wallet address or another indicator published by the researchers who analysed it. Normal code doesn't contain these, so a match means the file is very likely infected. The list comes from meSingh/polinrider-scanner (MIT license) and is bundled with devsweep, so it works offline. The campaign changes its markers every few weeks, so code/hidden-padding is what catches new variants. Markdown files are skipped, since security write-ups quote these strings."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The file contains a string proven to come from the PolinRider malware campaign: an obfuscator marker, a campaign tag, an XOR key, a wallet address or another indicator published by the researchers who analysed it. Normal code doesn't contain these, so a match means the file is very likely infected. The list comes from meSingh/polinrider-scanner (MIT license) and is bundled with devsweep, so it works offline. The campaign changes its markers every few weeks, so code/hidden-padding is what catches new variants. Markdown files are skipped, since security write-ups quote these strings.\n\nHow to fix: Don't build, run or open the project in your editor until it is cleaned. Remove the injected code, check the file's git history for when and how it was added, and check the other files that commit changed. If you built or ran the project, treat your machine as compromised: rotate tokens and passwords from a clean device. If the file is a security tool or a list of indicators, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json.",
                "markdown": "The file contains a string proven to come from the PolinRider malware campaign: an obfuscator marker, a campaign tag, an XOR key, a wallet address or another indicator published by the researchers who analysed it. Normal code doesn't contain these, so a match means the file is very likely infected. The list comes from meSingh/polinrider-scanner (MIT license) and is bundled with devsweep, so it works offline. The campaign changes its markers every few weeks, so code/hidden-padding is what catches new variants. Markdown files are skipped, since security write-ups quote these strings.\n\n## How to fix\n\nDon't build, run or open the project in your editor until it is cleaned. Remove the injected code, check the file's git history for when and how it was added, and check the other files that commit changed. If you built or ran the project, treat your machine as compromised: rotate tokens and passwords from a clean device. If the file is a security tool or a list of indicators, accept the finding in devsweep.config.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "code"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "originalUriBaseIds": {
        "%SRCROOT%": {
          "description": {
            "text": "The repository root."
          }
        }
      },
      "columnKind": "utf16CodeUnits",
      "invocations": [
        {
          "executionSuccessful": true
        }
      ],
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "workflow/untrusted-checkout",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Job \"preview\" runs on pull_request_target and checks out ref \"${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}\", so code from any fork can run with this repository's secrets and write token."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": ".github/workflows/pr-preview.yml",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 14,
                  "startColumn": 16,
                  "endLine": 14,
                  "endColumn": 57
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.5"
          }
